#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338" :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "7f4861fe-a783-5874-a22b-b4644257ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3339" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0),$ if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{ t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\:  1\\right\\rangle \\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨1,t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & = \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/assets/cc9e1eaf1dc8655565a94b7ef0146dc2bb458cc7376828b19352acc01ddbeaad.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "8d58f131-7260-5346-9a88-60862c0a1b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3340" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{ t}, \\: e^{- t} \\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,-1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adcf0a33-58ff-5c6f-9db0-2338f29becfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359731} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca36da4-9c28-56c8-997f-bf786abee190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359732} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c7d34b-c25a-5428-8ee0-c1959cae06f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359733} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8439ce-189c-5040-9908-63da18191d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359734} {:answer/id #uuid "a05e3edb-7e8e-57ba-a448-beece5846792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c7d34b-c25a-5428-8ee0-c1959cae06f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359733} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc1494de-5bd4-5937-8164-8ebbc73c4adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2282cba5-0247-5d06-9d27-a008f1535719" :question/math-academy-id "q-132105" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{cos}^{2}⁡s+2{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡s+2{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d03bb83c-ae8b-5801-9a64-9ebcffe18262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359738} {:answer/id #uuid "27412797-7dea-54d7-88c5-e908ae84a43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359739} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca16f3b-45d5-594b-ac3d-671bfaf67656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359740} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf9b47e-e00f-5d5c-a01d-7d5038cffb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359741} {:answer/id #uuid "d3686bab-c21e-5d91-81b8-c6319f40a50e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf9b47e-e00f-5d5c-a01d-7d5038cffb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359741} :answer-field/id #uuid "6526d23b-95c2-55cd-b8e7-c5f6943b2643" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41406066-0d4c-5ab4-ae88-9853e3376928" :question/math-academy-id "q-132110" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s})}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{s}+{cos}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}})}=4\\sqrt{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c392cd6f-3a69-576d-8921-68c0b89cf493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359745} {:answer/id #uuid "06ef2e42-e66d-5c57-908d-d5d361e54c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359746} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb96d4f-007e-5edf-9407-ba107ff8fcd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359747} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8faefe-90c9-5934-b966-486bebae13b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359748} {:answer/id #uuid "8acf5ca8-33a5-5bab-87f9-f27c9d61f55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c8faefe-90c9-5934-b966-486bebae13b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359748} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3a5335-82b9-5638-9237-d171488e70e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "737247d2-cfc0-5a76-b6fc-cde80c2d39de" :question/math-academy-id "q-132112" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(\\pi )$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d79041-205b-564e-8cb9-08efb1a51d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359752} {:answer/id #uuid "c46724a0-9e5b-5a1b-86d7-e0a90d011d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359753} {:answer/id #uuid "5640273b-9b42-584b-819b-2150c4eb85c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359754} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9301ba-d668-5d91-82ea-40d597a13d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359755} {:answer/id #uuid "a99d331a-4459-5d43-8af7-df13ffd64339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c46724a0-9e5b-5a1b-86d7-e0a90d011d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359753} :answer-field/id #uuid "83adfb6e-7423-5724-ac24-e55143fa15f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "399b70b2-c26b-5787-bbea-067efe659b98" :question/math-academy-id "q-132128" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =4⟨2(0),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =4⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times 8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-32k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{32} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4ca2c3-280f-5894-875c-5c0edbbf0679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359759} {:answer/id #uuid "2a679674-895b-52e3-b448-3a6d086551e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359760} {:answer/id #uuid "976cd78e-05a7-5931-a573-c47f7eafbd1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359761} {:answer/id #uuid "e93c1beb-25f9-51d1-951a-268c51bc02ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359762} {:answer/id #uuid "a623b501-3411-5ed9-bd9a-20b4442869a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a679674-895b-52e3-b448-3a6d086551e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359760} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d88c28b-9121-5ba5-9913-b4af417f5612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa158f76-5410-5104-9039-550ce8f90792" :question/math-academy-id "q-132130" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e0ac57-73d2-51a7-8942-2aaf5928ed9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359766} {:answer/id #uuid "931a23c3-fdf4-53a3-916e-f1762a47c587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359767} {:answer/id #uuid "cc50ba91-6f74-5966-ac6a-42ad2c634fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359768} {:answer/id #uuid "40c96597-5f0b-542b-80bc-7c3a19beb22d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359769} {:answer/id #uuid "cd975c9e-348d-546c-b2f4-1d8da0b57a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06e0ac57-73d2-51a7-8942-2aaf5928ed9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359766} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf9c8f9-d885-56ea-ab9d-feb7e51adfbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e19d212-99bc-5ec6-ba7f-76b0f4f4e8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132204" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩,$ since the point $P(0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "788f4a87-bfc3-5976-b4f1-bb14205248cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,-2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359773} {:answer/id #uuid "057fc5ba-3762-57a9-bc66-3fcef26e1d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,4⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359774} {:answer/id #uuid "604da060-e8cc-532a-a513-c77ceffb3f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-2,4⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359775} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4605b6-c47f-5a53-a750-f9bf49c9d12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359776} {:answer/id #uuid "d6123931-e82b-5a20-afd9-aa0bd8b3a4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,-2⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "788f4a87-bfc3-5976-b4f1-bb14205248cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨3,-2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359773} :answer-field/id #uuid "b463491b-d65b-51ec-8dcc-461c6fdb8c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "917351e8-3e93-5c8c-b787-a39fdea6cdc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132210" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+2⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+⟨2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7d0f-ef9d-56c7-b8ac-41f49fe36560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359780} {:answer/id #uuid "fc760af9-2de1-5910-8c48-81c477723569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359781} {:answer/id #uuid "682a9c74-e02d-56b0-ab53-052222cefc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359782} {:answer/id #uuid "a94e181c-ca3b-59f1-be43-075fd2a4c7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359783} {:answer/id #uuid "d87d4601-f5f1-5b7d-b85d-69971d250dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94e181c-ca3b-59f1-be43-075fd2a4c7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359783} :answer-field/id #uuid "e758c140-5a89-5d34-92b7-14516efe1051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df705f9d-a2f0-57c1-9293-5cf4d9e544fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-35114" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b0d034-c839-50d9-943f-bbbce1e02764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118342} {:answer/id #uuid "dbef72e2-3c3d-5b8b-a8fb-3011dbcb0854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118343} {:answer/id #uuid "fa46776a-29ac-5c01-a624-25f23b026b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118344} {:answer/id #uuid "5611cd21-cebf-563b-b8fb-068244089f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118345} {:answer/id #uuid "5addce22-c902-5f5a-9126-32945359443c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b0d034-c839-50d9-943f-bbbce1e02764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118342} :answer-field/id #uuid "be13805f-5f42-5cef-83d1-6122b41b23d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7abe7733-3947-526c-99df-24f7379a5dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-35234" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0248a6-d752-51fe-9341-6ef9d1b98f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359787} {:answer/id #uuid "b7482c83-6e34-52c4-8e97-d128ce69ef73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359788} {:answer/id #uuid "69ea459b-5aa4-5457-83b2-86e89add2c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359789} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa44a25-bfcb-5e96-bfe9-e191bb7416f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359790} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc57fcc-f5b4-5184-8590-7a01fda22ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0248a6-d752-51fe-9341-6ef9d1b98f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359787} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8499c4a-b922-5211-8821-9193f113ea5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a75f579-8ee0-5607-99e3-d5f80c6111fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64732" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3034a6e-67ed-5c41-9817-d96de6cb8d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359794} {:answer/id #uuid "a048a76f-88b6-5380-8828-7a623150cbd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359795} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c69805-a374-5f80-aeb7-953618d64989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359796} {:answer/id #uuid "b367ab11-08ab-5645-8aba-60737936b8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359797} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2b9eb9-7d2a-5d23-aacc-4f60dffa1d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3034a6e-67ed-5c41-9817-d96de6cb8d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359794} :answer-field/id #uuid "941a71bf-a7f5-5dc4-941d-2574a5f7c29d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10c6bbff-8da1-5a79-9fe0-55dfb246fa04" :question/math-academy-id "q-64738" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{25})}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "debccc52-f963-5379-8b1a-86b31580c2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359801} {:answer/id #uuid "7fff7941-c4b3-5e8c-b317-f97d6b4ed220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359802} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5756c2-5310-5d5c-92d9-53a20fc16dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359803} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5b10e5-7637-5c34-8764-fe163edd2055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359804} {:answer/id #uuid "87be91eb-8b6a-5a21-8299-877e02225dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debccc52-f963-5379-8b1a-86b31580c2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359801} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c3146c-10db-5d80-af47-1c972475df55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a574c4-fd0d-57cc-b972-6174b753f17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64836" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is $\\,T(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+R(\\frac{\\pi }{2})N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩=⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2a1b7b9-a5a1-512d-a5e6-26a1b0c0fcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359808} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe4e281-53a3-5f83-abad-8611b20d89b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359809} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f00530-abf1-5f6f-a023-69f58f64c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359810} {:answer/id #uuid "68e1b396-4504-52b0-99a9-854348ff9b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359811} {:answer/id #uuid "92a27b2b-9653-53ac-a288-bf4493224d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68e1b396-4504-52b0-99a9-854348ff9b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359811} :answer-field/id #uuid "823fcc0d-2b6a-5a5c-897f-b9188b5bb771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4521979f-e782-519a-bada-dd0881c64439" :question/math-academy-id "q-97593" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84df81f6-3e54-528a-b6cf-3992562b10c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359815} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca2262b-4b0f-5dc8-88da-1dec59056be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359816} {:answer/id #uuid "b4447a5c-768f-5647-822c-5d2c8bb84083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359817} {:answer/id #uuid "3f78124d-52d5-5972-b00c-001c137051cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359818} {:answer/id #uuid "95ff2f36-ecd7-510b-834d-383e6b07f3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4447a5c-768f-5647-822c-5d2c8bb84083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359817} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1c74ac-36bf-510b-bebb-39ebcfb0f08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b6d4e9d-2bd2-557f-873b-90eaa73125fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97594" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d446cea7-8bb9-59f8-870a-6b25e344bebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359822} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb767f0-57f5-5e48-badd-dc14414fb033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359823} {:answer/id #uuid "f07f8785-9418-58ed-b5d5-8456abd73ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359824} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccd1906-8c0e-58fb-8b13-29ecd27de00f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359825} {:answer/id #uuid "4228ebe7-4faf-520a-8901-acb5b3fd5180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d446cea7-8bb9-59f8-870a-6b25e344bebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359822} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a9c127-9716-543c-ba54-5120d2a3486c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ec30503-8a67-54c0-8a09-5a663e850850" :question/math-academy-id "q-98668" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ),\\,{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\pi },\\,-{e}^{\\pi }⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times {e}^{\\pi }⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi })}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\,0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ \\,0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi })}^{3}}{\\,2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
